WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.190 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in this lecture we will move to the  opposite side of the world from the  mediterranean  while in the west the new roman empire  was expanding  in the east another great empire the  chinese han was also spreading  just like in the west the han empire  came into conflict with tribal people as  it expanded imposed new rules about  family life and stimulated rebellions  here in the east we will follow the  fortunes of two sisters in vietnam who  rebelled against the powerful han empire  it's difficult to write about trung  truck and her sister trung yi  because the sources of information are  few  and the contemporary texts were written  by the chinese  but i think the effort is worth it  because even today the trung sisters  have become symbols of vietnamese  identity and their struggle for freedom  when i traveled in vietnam and saw their  statues and festivals i wondered who  they were and what they did  here i can tell you what they did  who they were as women remains elusive  but what matters most is how they became  symbols of freedom for the people of  vietnam  i'll begin their story in the year 25 in  the red river delta in vietnam  where troch and yi grew up  this region had been conquered by the  han dynasty of china more than a hundred  years earlier but life in the delta was  about to change  pulling the region into rebellion  first let's look at life in the delta to  get an idea of traditional vietnam  culture that was about to change  the tribes of this delta had lived here  for centuries and they prospered because  of the richness of the river  the red river rises in china and rushes  along for some 750 miles down through  mountain gorges before it spreads into  the delta  this triangular region of some 5 000  square miles contains rich lowlands as  the river system empties into the gulf  of tonkin near hanoi  the region is full of mangrove swamps  many species of water birds and vicious  saltwater crocodiles  villagers built houses on stilts to keep  out the water  and the animals this region had been  ruled by legendary kings in the hung  dynasty who had ruled from about 2800  bce  these rulers tattooed crocodiles on  their thighs to capture the courage of  the fierce beasts and to serve as tokens  to keep the animals away  recognizing these tattoos the chinese  for centuries referred to vietnam as the  land of the crocodile men  the kings lived at the borders of the  lowland delta and the neighboring hills  so they could control both regions  trung truck and trung yi were related to  the legendary hung kings on their  maternal side  this noble relationship gave them some  license to rule  and as we will see they would take  advantage of it  the villagers in the delta prospered  because of the rich rice fields that  they irrigated from the rising waters of  the red river the chinese had perfected  the domestication and cultivation of  rice in wet patties some 10 000 years  earlier  and the spread of rice down to the red  river delta had been lost in the mists  of time  here in vietnam the cultivation had been  firmly established and the delta  produced two abundant crops a year  people cultivated the rice helped by  their water buffalos  the people were called the loch by the  chinese and the name refers to the  vietnamese way of irrigating their rice  fields  the villages also grew rich on trade  that extended north to china and south  to the mekong delta and beyond  besides their abundant rice the  vietnamese traded their natural  resources like copper iron gold silver  and jade  they even grew rich exporting cowrie  shells much prized by the chinese as  symbols of fertility and luck  the loch tribes had learned to cast  bronze in about a thousand bce and still  used bronze tools and weapons in the  year 25.  today archaeologists  call this bronze age in vietnam the  dongasan culture  their most striking artifact was a  bronze drum that they cast and decorated  beautifully  some of the drums were of an  extraordinary size they could weigh up  to 159 pounds and required huge amounts  of smelted copper  but the drums sounded the heartbeat of  the villages and clans that spread  throughout the delta and the neighboring  highlands  they were played for feasts and funerals  and they called the clan's warriors  together for war  as we will see  soon the two noble trung sisters would  sound the drums for war  to begin to understand the origins of  the war we first must understand  something about the social life of the  vietnamese and the status of their women  traditional viet society was based on  clans not on nuclear families and within  these clans women carried a great deal  of sovereignty  in fact some anthropologists consider  the dongsan culture to be matriarchal  with a child's mother being much more  important than ties of paternity  young women were free to choose lovers  at spring festivals and if a girl became  pregnant during these events the young  man was to go to her clan marry her and  be accepted as a son-in-law  if she did not get pregnant she was free  to choose another young man  mothers were responsible for raising the  children and proverbs echo this ancient  time as they say a spoiled child is  attributable to the mother or  grandmother  or if a child does wrong it's the mother  who bears responsibility  this responsibility extended into  adulthood  and women and grandmothers were  regularly consulted about the plans and  fortunes of their clan  choc ani's mother was a widow who raised  her two daughters on her own  and this was solidly within the  matriarchal tradition  she taught the girls skills usual for  both women and men  the girls learned to raise silkworms and  they also learned to make roasted  silkworm poopie which is a popular dish  in north vietnam today  but the girls were also brought up to  lead outside the home  their mother also had her daughters  trained in the martial arts sword  fighting and archery and they learned to  ride the war elephants that accompanied  the armies through the jungles  according to legend trung trock the  elder daughter was very heroic  courageous and good at bold stratagem  in 111 bc  expanding han dynasty of the chinese  extended southward and conquered most of  north vietnam  the chinese ruled the rich red river  delta province as well as the two  regions north and south of the delta so  they controlled the whole gulf of tonkin  for over a century the chinese rule  rested lightly on the land of the lock  the clan system remained in place and  women continued to wield a good deal of  authority  the drums still sounded through the  hills and clans gathered together to  celebrate and sometimes fight among each  other  the chinese influence was felt most  strongly in the prosperity that grew as  trade from the han empire extended all  the way to the west connecting to the  roman empire across the famous silk road  the chinese presence showed in garrisons  placed in the provinces but they were  only a token force  however  in 25 ce  events far to the north in the han  empire created changes that would affect  the provinces far to the south in  vietnam  what was going on in the north  a brief revolution that overthrew the  han emperor for about 16 years caused a  backlash that rippled down into the  delta changing the status quo  the han empire that had been established  in 206 bce  experienced a brief lapse in rule when a  rebellion introduced a new dynasty the  jinn  this dynasty lasted only from 9 ce to 23  ce and was ruled only by one emperor  the newly restored han dynasty is called  the eastern han because its emperors  moved the capital from shang east  to luoyang the eastern han empire  continued for another 200 years  until 220c  you

